{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-871de1abdd649757","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9011\n3\tepic_count: 10\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9011\n11\t\n12\tAdopt structured JSON logging across RHOAI components. The strategy is a\n13\tclassic framework-then-consumers shape: one platform-wide logging convention\n14\t(HLR-1) unblocks independent per-component adoptions (HLR-2..HLR-8), with\n15\tproduct documentation closing it out (HLR-9). The strategy explicitly states\n16\tcomponent adoptions are independent of each other once the convention exists,\n17\tand its Open Questions table is empty — no Investigation epics are needed.\n18\t\n19\t## Epic List\n20\t\n21\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n22\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 | Publish platform structured JSON logging convention | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E002 | Adopt structured JSON logging in Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E003 | Adopt structured JSON logging in the KServe controller | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E004 | Adopt structured JSON logging in serving runtimes | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E005 | Adopt structured JSON logging in Data Science Pipelines | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E006 | Adopt structured JSON logging in Model Registry | Implementation | Model Registry | P1 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E007 | Adopt structured JSON logging in TrustyAI services | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E008 | Adopt structured JSON logging in notebook-controller | Implementation | Workbenches | P1 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E009 | Adopt structured JSON logging in Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P2 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E010 | Document the structured log format and support queries | Implementation | RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n33\t\n34\tNotes on the list:\n35\t\n36\t- E010 carries `implementation_type: docs-authoring` (Red Hat docs content\n37\t  process). Its priority is P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception:\n38\t  strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` maps to P1, even though HLR-9 is P2.\n39\t- All seven affected components exist in `.context/architecture-context/`\n40\t  (rhoai-3.0) and are Konflux-onboarded, so no onboarding chain\n41\t  (repo/license/konflux) epics are generated.\n42\t- HLR-3 covers both the KServe controller and the serving runtimes. The\n43\t  strategy's Affected Components list names only \"KServe\", but serving\n44\t  runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit — model execution images) are a separate\n45\t  canonical component per `.context/rhai-components.txt` and per the KServe\n46\t  architecture context (listed as a dependency, not part of KServe). Per the\n47\t  epic boundary rule (different component → separate epic), HLR-3 is covered\n48\t  by E003 (KServe) and E004 (Serving Runtimes), both owned by RHOAI Serving.\n49\t- The strategy provides no Impacted Teams table. Team assignments for E002\n50\t  through E009 are taken from the `Owning team` field of each component's\n51\t  architecture context file. The convention epic (E001) is platform-wide; it\n52\t  is assigned to the RHOAI Platform team and the documentation epic (E010) to\n53\t  RHOAI Documentation — both assumptions flagged in Health Warnings.\n54\t\n55\t## Dependency DAG\n56\t\n57\t```mermaid\n58\tgraph TD\n59\t    E001[E001: Logging convention] --> E002[E002: Dashboard]\n60\t    E001 --> E003[E003: KServe controller]\n61\t    E001 --> E004[E004: Serving runtimes]\n62\t    E001 --> E005[E005: Data Science Pipelines]\n63\t    E001 --> E006[E006: Model Registry]\n64\t    E001 --> E007[E007: TrustyAI]\n65\t    E001 --> E008[E008: notebook-controller]\n66\t    E001 --> E009[E009: Training Operator]\n67\t    E002 --> E010[E010: Documentation]\n68\t    E003 --> E010\n69\t    E004 --> E010\n70\t    E005 --> E010\n71\t    E006 --> E010\n72\t    E007 --> E010\n73\t    E008 --> E010\n74\t    E009 --> E010\n75\t```\n76\t\n77\t## DAG Justification\n78\t\n79\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n80\t|------|------|-----------|\n81\t| E001 → E002..E009 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer, serial) | The convention (schema, required fields, per-language library guidance) is the artifact every adoption builds against; no adoption can produce conformant output before it exists. |\n82\t| E001 → E010 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes the convention itself, not just adoptions. |\n83\t| E002..E009 → E010 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Docs describe what was built; example queries must reflect actual component output. |\n84\t| (none) between E002..E009 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts → parallel) | Strategy states adoptions are independent once the convention exists; each touches a different component/repo with no shared data structures or API surfaces. |\n85\t| (no konflux-onboarding edges) | Rule 10 | No onboarding epics exist — all components are already Konflux-onboarded per architecture context. |\n86\t\n87\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n88\t\n89\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n90\t|-----|----------|---------|\n91\t| HLR-1 (platform logging convention published) | P0 | E001 |\n92\t| HLR-2 (Dashboard conformant) | P0 | E002 |\n93\t| HLR-3 (KServe controller and serving runtimes conformant) | P0 | E003, E004 |\n94\t| HLR-4 (Data Science Pipelines conformant) | P1 | E005 |\n95\t| HLR-5 (Model Registry conformant) | P1 | E006 |\n96\t| HLR-6 (TrustyAI conformant) | P1 | E007 |\n97\t| HLR-7 (notebook-controller conformant) | P1 | E008 |\n98\t| HLR-8 (Training Operator conformant) | P2 | E009 |\n99\t| HLR-9 (documentation of format and queries) | P2 | E010 |\n100\t\n101\tAll nine HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. Priority inheritance:\n102\tE001 is directly P0 (HLR-1) and also blocks all P0 adoptions, so it is P0 by\n103\tboth routes.\n104\t\n105\t## Health Warnings\n106\t\n107\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n108\t   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n109\t   Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were derived from architecture context\n110\t   owning-team fields; epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR text.\n111\t   Human verification of team assignments is recommended.\n112\t2. **Assumed team ownership.** The convention epic (E001, team \"RHOAI\n113\t   Platform\") and the documentation epic (E010, team \"RHOAI Documentation\")\n114\t   have no team stated anywhere in the strategy or architecture context.\n115\t3. **Docs priority inversion (expected per rules).** E010 is P1 (docs-authoring\n116\t   exception: strategy priority Major → P1) although HLR-9 is P2. Additionally,\n117\t   Rule 11 blocks E010 on all Implementations, including the P2 Training\n118\t   Operator adoption (E009) — the P1 docs epic cannot complete until the\n119\t   lowest-priority adoption lands. If Training Operator slips, docs slip with\n120\t   it; consider descope options at planning time.\n121\t4. **Midstream rebase risk (not a dependency).** Most adoptions land in\n122\t   midstream forks of upstream projects (opendatahub-io/kserve,\n123\t   data-science-pipelines-operator, model-registry, kubeflow notebook-controller\n124\t   fork, training-operator). These are coordination risks, not DAG edges; no\n125\t   upstream contribution is required by the strategy.\n126\t5. **Serving runtimes scope breadth.** E004 spans multiple model-execution\n127\t   runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit) with heterogeneous logging stacks. The\n128\t   strategy treats them as one component; if per-runtime divergence proves\n129\t   large, the implementing team may need to split delivery internally.\n130\t\n131\t## Tiered Delivery\n132\t\n133\tNot applicable. There are no external dependencies with uncertain timing: the\n134\tconvention is internal work and all adoption targets are platform components.\n135\tThe natural staging (convention first, adoptions in parallel, docs last) is\n136\talready captured by the DAG.\n137\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9eb95d6a25ecc9e7","agent_id":"a4bfe979f8721182c","session_id":"3eca0caa-e3c9-4b4a-8579-02aaafb311df","uuid":"48816850-7d48-4c6f-8c24-35073413ce8a","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:50:24.183Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-871de1abdd649757","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9011 decomposition"}
