{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab2adb83dd887274","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9011\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: E010 frontmatter lists RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 as a direct dependency and\n10\t    the DAG Justification table lists the edge E001 -> E010 (Rule 11), but the mermaid\n11\t    Dependency DAG diagram omits the E001 --> E010 edge — frontmatter dependencies\n12\t    are inconsistent with the decomposition DAG diagram. Fix by adding the missing\n13\t    edge to the diagram (or, if the edge is intentionally redundant/transitive, removing\n14\t    E001 from E010 direct dependencies and from the justification table so all three\n15\t    representations agree).\n16\t- severity: minor\n17\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n18\t  description: E004 bundles three heterogeneous serving runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO,\n19\t    Caikit) with different logging stacks; already acknowledged in Health Warning\n20\t    5, but the implementing team may need an internal split if divergence proves large.\n21\terror: null\n22\t---\n23\t## Review Summary\n24\t\n25\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n26\tRecommendation: revise\n27\t\n28\tOne critical issue: the decomposition summary's mermaid DAG diagram omits\n29\tthe `E001 --> E010` edge that E010's frontmatter `dependencies` and the\n30\tsummary's own DAG Justification table both assert. Criterion 2 scores 0 by\n31\tthe auto-fail rule; all other criteria pass at full credit.\n32\t\n33\t## Criterion Details\n34\t\n35\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n36\t\n37\tAll nine HLRs map to at least one epic, and the traceability matrix is\n38\tpresent and complete: HLR-1→E001, HLR-2→E002, HLR-3→E003+E004, HLR-4→E005,\n39\tHLR-5→E006, HLR-6→E007, HLR-7→E008, HLR-8→E009, HLR-9→E010. No P0 or P1\n40\tgaps; P2 HLRs (HLR-8, HLR-9) are covered rather than deferred, which is\n41\tacceptable. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 blocks all P0 adoptions\n42\tand is itself P0. E010's P1 (despite HLR-9 being P2) is the correct\n43\tapplication of the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority\n44\tMajor → P1) — not flagged. The HLR-3 split across E003/E004 is a\n45\tcomponent-boundary split within a single HLR, not a priority collapse; no\n46\tepic bundles HLRs at different priority levels, so no deferrable work is\n47\tlocked behind higher-priority epics.\n48\t\n49\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n50\t\n51\t**Critical — frontmatter/diagram inconsistency on E001→E010.** E010's\n52\tfrontmatter `dependencies` lists RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 through\n53\tRHAISTRAT-9011-E009 (nine direct dependencies), and the summary's DAG\n54\tJustification table explicitly lists the edge `E001 → E010` (Rule 11,\n55\t\"Documentation describes the convention itself, not just adoptions\").\n56\tHowever, the mermaid diagram in the Dependency DAG section draws only\n57\t`E002 --> E010` through `E009 --> E010` — the `E001 --> E010` edge is\n58\tmissing. The diagram and the frontmatter therefore disagree on E010's\n59\tdirect dependency set. Per the cross-check consistency rule, a mismatch\n60\tbetween frontmatter dependencies and the decomposition DAG diagram is a\n61\tcritical issue. The fix is trivial (add one line to the mermaid diagram);\n62\tthe edge itself is valid and correctly justified.\n63\t\n64\tEverything else in the DAG is sound:\n65\t\n66\t- No circular dependencies; all referenced epics exist.\n67\t- All 16 drawn edges have matching frontmatter `dependencies` entries\n68\t  (only the reverse direction fails, for the edge above).\n69\t- E002..E009 are correctly parallel (Rule 14: different repos, no shared\n70\t  artifacts), matching the strategy's explicit statement that adoptions\n71\t  are independent once the convention exists.\n72\t- No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics\n73\t  exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints