{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8784074842cec546","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9011\n3\tepic_count: 10\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9011\n10\t\n11\tAdopt structured JSON logging across RHOAI components. The strategy is a\n12\tclassic framework-then-consumers shape: one platform-wide logging convention\n13\t(HLR-1) unblocks independent per-component adoptions (HLR-2..HLR-8), with\n14\tproduct documentation closing it out (HLR-9). The strategy explicitly states\n15\tcomponent adoptions are independent of each other once the convention exists,\n16\tand its Open Questions table is empty — no Investigation epics are needed.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 | Publish platform structured JSON logging convention | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E002 | Adopt structured JSON logging in Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E003 | Adopt structured JSON logging in the KServe controller | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E004 | Adopt structured JSON logging in serving runtimes | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E005 | Adopt structured JSON logging in Data Science Pipelines | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E006 | Adopt structured JSON logging in Model Registry | Implementation | Model Registry | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E007 | Adopt structured JSON logging in TrustyAI services | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E008 | Adopt structured JSON logging in notebook-controller | Implementation | Workbenches | P1 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E009 | Adopt structured JSON logging in Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P2 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9011-E010 | Document the structured log format and support queries | Implementation | RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n32\t\n33\tNotes on the list:\n34\t\n35\t- E010 carries `implementation_type: docs-authoring` (Red Hat docs content\n36\t  process). Its priority is P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception:\n37\t  strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` maps to P1, even though HLR-9 is P2.\n38\t- All seven affected components exist in `.context/architecture-context/`\n39\t  (rhoai-3.0) and are Konflux-onboarded, so no onboarding chain\n40\t  (repo/license/konflux) epics are generated.\n41\t- HLR-3 covers both the KServe controller and the serving runtimes. The\n42\t  strategy's Affected Components list names only \"KServe\", but serving\n43\t  runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit — model execution images) are a separate\n44\t  canonical component per `.context/rhai-components.txt` and per the KServe\n45\t  architecture context (listed as a dependency, not part of KServe). Per the\n46\t  epic boundary rule (different component → separate epic), HLR-3 is covered\n47\t  by E003 (KServe) and E004 (Serving Runtimes), both owned by RHOAI Serving.\n48\t- The strategy provides no Impacted Teams table. Team assignments for E002\n49\t  through E009 are taken from the `Owning team` field of each component's\n50\t  architecture context file. The convention epic (E001) is platform-wide; it\n51\t  is assigned to the RHOAI Platform team and the documentation epic (E010) to\n52\t  RHOAI Documentation — both assumptions flagged in Health Warnings.\n53\t\n54\t## Dependency DAG\n55\t\n56\t```mermaid\n57\tgraph TD\n58\t    E001[E001: Logging convention] --> E002[E002: Dashboard]\n59\t    E001 --> E003[E003: KServe controller]\n60\t    E001 --> E004[E004: Serving runtimes]\n61\t    E001 --> E005[E005: Data Science Pipelines]\n62\t    E001 --> E006[E006: Model Registry]\n63\t    E001 --> E007[E007: TrustyAI]\n64\t    E001 --> E008[E008: